  "overview": {
    "text": "Okun’s law is an observed economic pattern, named for economist Arthur Okun, linking unemployment with production: when unemployment rises above its estimated natural rate, real output often falls below estimated potential output. Economists use it in two main ways: to relate changes in output growth to changes in unemployment, or to compare the output gap with the unemployment gap. The pattern is useful for understanding economic slowdowns and estimating unused productive capacity. But it is not a guaranteed formula: estimates vary across methods and time periods, and the relationship is statistical rather than proof that one change directly causes the other. Potential output and the natural rate of unemployment must also be estimated.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://www.stlouisfed.org/publications/regional-economist/october-2013/output-and-unemployment-how-do-they-relate-today",
      "https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/speech/mishkin20070524a.htm",
      "https://www.brookings.edu/articles/labor-force-structure-potential-output-and-productivity/"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "Arthur Okun’s 1962 work connected unemployment with the gap between actual and potential output.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.stlouisfed.org/publications/regional-economist/october-2013/output-and-unemployment-how-do-they-relate-today",
        "https://www.brookings.edu/articles/labor-force-structure-potential-output-and-productivity/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The “output gap” is the difference between actual output and estimated potential output; the “unemployment gap” compares actual unemployment with an estimated natural rate.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/speech/mishkin20070524a.htm"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Okun’s original growth-rate estimate suggested that each percentage-point increase in real GNP growth went with an average 0.3-percentage-point decrease in unemployment.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.stlouisfed.org/publications/regional-economist/october-2013/output-and-unemployment-how-do-they-relate-today"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The relationship is an empirical guide, not proof that output changes directly cause unemployment changes.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.stlouisfed.org/publications/regional-economist/october-2013/output-and-unemployment-how-do-they-relate-today"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Productivity shifts and other structural changes can make the relationship less reliable, while estimates of potential output and the natural unemployment rate are uncertain.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/speech/mishkin20070524a.htm"
      ]
    }
  ],
